WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.999 --> 00:00:03.939
Thanks to CuriosityStream for supporting this
episode!

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:09.559
Go to CuriosityStream.com/microcosmos to start
streaming thousands of documentaries and non

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:11.086
fiction TV shows.

00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:20.210
This frontonia is swimming in a minefield.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:26.289
We can’t see all the dangers it’s evading,
but abruptly, the frontonia stops, and then

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:31.250
very, very slowly, begins to expand outward.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:36.879
In this moment, the frontonia’s entire body
seems to shift away from its placid demeanor.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:39.558
Gone is the swimming organism.

00:00:39.558 --> 00:00:43.730
Now we have something more strained.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.790
It’s like when a cat is staring off in the
distance with every muscle tensed and every

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.540
bit of fur standing on end.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:57.787
You know just by looking that something is
causing distress, but the source of the terror

00:00:57.787 --> 00:00:59.578
is invisible.

00:00:59.578 --> 00:01:03.000
But still you keep looking, because you want
to know—

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.735
you have to know—

00:01:04.735 --> 00:01:07.980
what has frozen this creature in alarm?

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.898
And why is it reacting this way?

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.320
The homalozoon we’re watching right now
has a lot in common with the frontonia we

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.708
were just observing.

00:01:31.708 --> 00:01:36.750
Like Frontonia and most of the organisms we’ll
be talking about today, the homalozoon is

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:43.299
a ciliate, a single-celled organism marked
by the hair-like structures around its body.

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:47.189
But this homalozoon isn’t dealing with invisible
threats.

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:51.094
It is the threat, on the hunt for food.

00:01:51.094 --> 00:01:56.750
And when it comes across this other ciliate,
a paramecium, the homalozoon has an invisible

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.470
weapon to help bring down its prey.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:06.729
But instead, the homalozoon flinches from
the paramecium as if it has been stung, because

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:09.931
in a sense, it has been.

00:02:09.931 --> 00:02:16.990
What we’ve just watched unfold is a battle
between extrusomes, a type of vesicle found

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.279
within single-celled organisms.

00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:26.213
Within the cell, extrusomes usually take on
crystal-like structures or are housed in capsules.

00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:30.879
But as we’ll see in a bit, when the time
comes to be used, extrusomes can be quite

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:34.120
dramatic to watch in action.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.010
What I’ve just told you doesn’t reveal
much about an extrusome, though.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.000
Is it an attack?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.378
A defense?

00:02:40.378 --> 00:02:42.378
Something else entirely?

00:02:42.378 --> 00:02:43.399
Yes.

00:02:43.399 --> 00:02:46.099
But more specifically, it depends.

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.566
Think of extrusomes sort of like a knife.

00:02:48.566 --> 00:02:53.036
It can be a weapon, sure, but it can also
be used to feed or to craft.

00:02:53.036 --> 00:02:58.180
It all depends on the nature of the blade
and the person wielding it.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.629
Which is to say that there are a lot of different
types of knives in the world,

00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:05.800
and also there are many types of extrusomes.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.629
And while we cannot provide an exhaustive
display of all of the extrusomes known to

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.728
scientists right now, we can at least observe
a few of them.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:24.231
Let’s start with the extrusome wielded by
our hungry homalozoon from earlier, which

00:03:24.231 --> 00:03:29.304
is called a toxicyst because, it is
loaded with toxins.

00:03:29.304 --> 00:03:33.830
We have seen toxicysts in action in various
ciliates throughout our journey through the

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:39.303
microcosmos, and perhaps our best view has
come from the dileptus.

00:03:39.303 --> 00:03:44.349
All along that trunk-like structure are hundreds
of toxicysts.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:50.909
And as the dileptus hunts for food, it sweeps
its trunk from side to side so that any prey

00:03:50.909 --> 00:03:57.269
that comes in contact with the trunk will
also come in contact with those toxicysts.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:03.208
For the ciliates that have them, the main
task of toxicysts is to help catch prey.

00:04:03.208 --> 00:04:08.180
When not in use, they are stored 
neatly within capsules, lying in wait.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:13.890
But should an unsuspecting meal come too close,
predators like this lacrymaria will extend

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:19.940
their toxicysts out from their body and towards
their prey to paralyze it.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:25.290
But look, different predators have different needs,
so their toxicysts may look different and

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.272
be wielded differently.

00:04:27.272 --> 00:04:32.850
Some organisms might rely more on the paralytic
activity of the toxins, while others use the

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.400
toxicysts to physically hold onto their prey.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.540
There are also extrusomes that are similar
to toxicysts, but not quite similar enough

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:42.974
to be grouped with them.

00:04:42.974 --> 00:04:49.660
Like rhabdocysts, rod-shaped extrusomes found
on karyorelictid ciliates like this Trachelocercid,

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:53.410
that shoot like arrows into their prey.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:57.759
And then there are suctorians, which look
very unassuming here.

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:03.186
They’re the round bodies sticking out from
a stalk in the middle of your screen right now.

00:05:03.186 --> 00:05:09.264
As we change our perspective though, we can
start to make out the tentacles lining their bodies.

00:05:09.264 --> 00:05:15.546
And among those tentacles are extrusomes called
haptocysts, which have a round knob at the end.

00:05:15.546 --> 00:05:22.419
As unthreatening as suctorians appear, those
haptocysts were alble to grab ahold of some

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:25.421
unsuspecting creature that got too close.

00:05:25.421 --> 00:05:30.440
Not only were the haptocysts able to puncture
the prey’s body, they were able to act as

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:37.419
a highway back to the predator, creating a
connection that ultimately feeds the suctorian.

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:43.400
But as we saw earlier, the homalozoon wielded
a toxicyst against the paramecium, but the

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.584
paramecium also had the ability to fight back.

00:05:47.584 --> 00:05:51.817
The paramecium rely on a set
of extrusomes called trichocysts.

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:58.962
In this clip, James—our master of microscopes—has
added some diluted ethyl alcohol onto a slide.

00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:04.240
The paramecia are trying to huddle in the
pockets of water where there’s less alcohol,

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.360
but as they come in contact with more and
more of the alcohol, they begin to release

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.180
their trichocysts.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.810
It might be a little difficult to see, but
up close, with some patience and a careful

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.569
eye, you may be able to see it better.

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:23.919
Ignore the bubbles that are emerging from
the edge of the paramecium, apparently that's

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:27.599
something that can just happen as a paramecium
sits on the slide for a while.

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:33.260
But look around them, at the long threads
emerging from the side of the paramecium,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:36.639
which seem to overwhelm the ciliate.

00:06:36.639 --> 00:06:42.300
Paramecium and their trichocysts are perhaps
the best-studied extrusomes among all ciliates.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:48.160
Unlike toxicysts, which are usually localized
to one part of the predator’s body, trichocysts

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.220
are usually found throughout the length of
the organism’s body.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:58.190
But they do have special docking sites that
keep the trichocysts situated right below

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.404
the plasma membrane.

00:07:00.404 --> 00:07:05.510
When a stimulus like a predator (or alcohol)
comes along, the membrane of the trichocyst

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:10.927
will begin to fuse with the paramecium’s
membrane, creating an opening from which the

00:07:10.927 --> 00:07:13.723
trichocyst can discharge.

00:07:13.723 --> 00:07:19.129
While paramecium have the best characterized
trichocysts of all, other species do have

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:24.610
trichocysts as well, including the frontonia
we showed you in the beginning of this episode.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:31.139
In fact, we saw them in action, set off by
the cleaning chemicals that James had added

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:32.360
to the water.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:38.000
He repeated this multiple times, adding drops
of this invisible danger so we can observe

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.259
the frontonia’s trichocysts.

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:46.949
Again and again, you can see how the cell
stops and seems instantaneously surrounded

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:54.610
by a mass of thin threads, similar in structure
to the paramecium’s trichocysts we saw earlier.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:59.599
The extrusomes we’ve talked about so far
have clear roles in the eternal battle between

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:01.466
predator and prey.

00:08:01.466 --> 00:08:04.604
Some defend, some attack.

00:08:04.604 --> 00:08:07.333
But some extrusomes have a murkier purpose.

00:08:07.333 --> 00:08:09.338
Like mucosyts.

00:08:09.338 --> 00:08:14.240
We’re showing them here in euglena, which
are flagellates, but they’re found in ciliates

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.483
as well like loxophyllum.

00:08:16.483 --> 00:08:22.270
They might be a way to get food, helping their
organisms create a sticky cell surface that

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:23.672
acts as a trap.

00:08:23.672 --> 00:08:28.835
But they might also be a way to help the organism
build a cyst, a tool for movement.

00:08:28.835 --> 00:08:35.123
Their exact purpose remains unclear, and who
knows, maybe they have a range of uses.

00:08:35.123 --> 00:08:40.209
And with so many more types of extrusomes
that exist in the ciliate world, there are

00:08:40.209 --> 00:08:45.938
so many opportunities for us to understand
more how these organisms interact

00:08:45.938 --> 00:08:47.546
with the world around them.

00:08:47.546 --> 00:08:53.260
These are all organelles perched along the
borders, an expression of hunger or a demand

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:59.910
for protection or something more mysterious,
waiting for a signal that says it’s their

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.363
time to eject out into the world.

